comments.body:
In commit "wallet: migration, make sqlite creation robust against failures" (da055ca4bc59df70d034e7f528165f4d13ed06bf)

When I first read this I thought the loop only removed files this code had created, but it deletes files it finds at the destination that shouldn't normally be there. Maybe the comment could say so more directly:

```suggestion
    // Check for destination files that should never be present normally, but could exist if an outside process created them
    // or an earlier migration was interrupted. Return an error if they look like wallet databases, otherwise delete them. In
    // particular, delete a leftover journal, which SQLite would roll back into the moved database when opening it, corrupting it.
```

Alternately, I think i would be safer and simpler to return an error for any file found here, dropping the database checks. As far as I can tell, failures that are reported (like running out of disk space) already clean up their journal, so a leftover one can only come from the process dying during an earlier migration followed by a manual restore, or from something outside Bitcoin Core. That's rare, and a user who hits it has already been recovering by hand, so an error naming the file would be easy to act on. Current approach seems ok though.
